WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.490
Picture paying a friend back.

2
00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.194
You send 20.00, your app says Sent, and you text them: done.

3
00:00:08.194 --> 00:00:12.022
They open their app a second later, and the money isn't there.

4
00:00:12.022 --> 00:00:13.009
Then it appears.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.809
For a moment, their app showed a world where you hadn't paid.

6
00:00:16.809 --> 00:00:20.714
How does a database spread over many machines end up showing that?

7
00:00:20.714 --> 00:00:21.839
Hold that question.

8
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.675
Think of bank branches in different towns.

9
00:00:24.675 --> 00:00:28.660
Each clerk stamps deposit slips with the branch's own wall clock.

10
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.811
An auditor notes the time the audit starts, and counts every slip stamped before it.

11
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.961
If the clocks agree, that works.

12
00:00:35.961 --> 00:00:37.739
But clocks never quite agree.

13
00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:43.193
A slip stamped by a fast clock can look newer than an audit that really started after it.

14
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:44.929
Now the lab's version.

15
00:00:44.929 --> 00:00:51.521
Two servers: settle pays batch 1 of the nightly payout, and its clock runs ten milliseconds fast.

16
00:00:51.521 --> 00:00:58.045
audit-0 runs an audit, a report that checks the books, and its clock runs ten milliseconds slow.

17
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.424
Settle commits batch 1 at timestamp 10, its own clock reading.

18
00:01:02.424 --> 00:01:06.171
At 8 milliseconds of true time, the client hears: paid.

19
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:09.133
One millisecond later, the audit starts.

20
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:13.974
Its clock reads minus 1, so it reads everything stamped up to minus 1.

21
00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:16.948
Batch 1, stamped 10, looks like the future.

22
00:01:16.948 --> 00:01:19.023
The audit reads payouts: zero.

23
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:19.484
Well.

24
00:01:19.484 --> 00:01:23.735
The batch was paid before the audit began, and the audit missed it.

25
00:01:23.735 --> 00:01:26.781
That's your friend's empty app, with timestamps.

26
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.574
There are fixes that make every writer pay.

27
00:01:29.574 --> 00:01:36.222
A head office can hand out every stamp by phone, or every clerk can wait out the clock's error before saying done.

28
00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:40.420
But what if writers don't wait at all, and the reader handles the doubt?

29
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:47.506
That's what CockroachDB does, a SQL database that runs on ordinary servers with ordinary clocks.

30
00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:51.709
It promises a bound on how far any two clocks may disagree.

31
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:58.932
So a reader at timestamp T knows that anything stamped above T plus that bound was written after it began.

32
00:01:58.932 --> 00:02:05.403
Anything in between is uncertain: it may have committed first, on a server whose clock runs ahead.

33
00:02:05.403 --> 00:02:11.345
In the lab the bound is 20 milliseconds, so the audit's window runs from minus 1 up to 19.

34
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.040
Batch 1 is stamped 10.

35
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.140
Inside the window, or outside?

36
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.169
And what should the audit do?

37
00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:18.289
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:22.020
Inside.

39
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:28.072
The audit can't tell whether that payment came before it or after, so it can't skip it.

40
00:02:28.072 --> 00:02:33.568
It moves its own read timestamp up to 10, and reads again: payouts 1, ledger 1.

41
00:02:33.568 --> 00:02:34.403
One restart.

42
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.475
The audit took 4 milliseconds instead of 2, and the writer still answered at 8.

43
00:02:39.475 --> 00:02:45.707
It's the auditor who finds a slip stamped just after the audit began, and starts over to count it in.

44
00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:50.775
That window is called the uncertainty interval, and the bound is the max offset.

45
00:02:50.775 --> 00:02:55.644
The top of the window stays put when the read moves up, so the restarts must end.

46
00:02:55.867 --> 00:03:03.367
The writes themselves go in as intents, provisional values, plus one transaction record that says commit or abort.

47
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:11.261
With parallel commits, the transaction counts as done once every intent has reached agreement: one round instead of two.

48
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:12.962
Now, the fine print.

49
00:03:12.962 --> 00:03:20.746
The max offset is 500 milliseconds by default, and 250 is suggested for clusters spread over several regions.

50
00:03:20.746 --> 00:03:31.244
The bound has to hold, so a server whose clock is off from at least half the others by 80% of it shuts itself down, rather than give wrong answers.

51
00:03:31.433 --> 00:03:35.102
If clocks drift past it anyway, real-time order can break.

52
00:03:35.102 --> 00:03:37.568
And here the branch picture breaks too.

53
00:03:37.568 --> 00:03:39.656
Clerks would recount for seconds.

54
00:03:39.656 --> 00:03:48.953
Servers do it for milliseconds, thousands of times a second, so clock error becomes a cost you can measure, paid by reads of recently written keys.

55
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.309
So the moral of the story: the bound on the clock's error, not the clock's reading, sets the price.

56
00:03:55.309 --> 00:03:56.296
Know your bound.

57
00:03:56.567 --> 00:03:57.801
One quick check.

58
00:03:57.801 --> 00:04:02.352
The audit reads at minus 1 again, with its window up to 19.

59
00:04:02.352 --> 00:04:05.438
Suppose batch 1 were stamped 23 instead.

60
00:04:05.438 --> 00:04:06.672
Restart, or not?

61
00:04:06.672 --> 00:04:07.907
Pause and think.

62
00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:11.911
No restart.

63
00:04:11.911 --> 00:04:17.120
23 is above the window's top, so it was surely written after the audit began.

64
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.232
The audit can skip it with a clear conscience.
